1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,500
Los Angeles gets more than two thousand
earthquakes in three months.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:10,500
This twelve-dollar motion chip can feel the
bigger ones.

3
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:15,000
It just has no idea what it is feeling. A raw
acceleration number means nothing on its own.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:21,000
But in Los Angeles the ground truth is public.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Minutes after every quake, the USGS publishes
its magnitude and its location.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:33,000
So the device waits, feels a shake, then asks
the catalog what that shake actually was.

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Match enough of them and it learns its own
site-specific response — for free.

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The UNO Q is two computers.

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,000
The microcontroller watches the accelerometer
a hundred times a second, and runs the classic
seismology trigger.

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The Linux side handles WiFi, queries the USGS,
correlates, and serves the dashboard.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Here it is running on the board, on its own.

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Every ten seconds it reports its noise floor.
Now watch when I tap the desk.

13
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Peak acceleration, duration, dominant
frequency — all three measured in real time.

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:25,000
And it correctly reports no match in the
catalog: a tap on a desk is not an earthquake.

15
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:32,000
This next part is a replay, not a live
recording.

16
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,000
It feeds the last day of real cataloged quakes
through the pipeline as synthetic shakes.

17
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:46,000
So you can watch in thirty seconds what takes
weeks in the field.

18
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Red is what the device thinks. Colour is what
really happened. The dashed line is its error.

19
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Now the part most demos skip.

20
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:10,000
The dashboard's residual is measured on the
very points the model was fitted on, and it is
handed the true distance. That flatters it.

21
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Every detection is journalled with what the
model predicted before it learned that point.

22
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Replaying that journal gives the out-of-sample
error — several times worse.

23
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,000
It detects, it does not predict. One station
cannot triangulate.

24
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:30,000
But a cheap sensor plus open public data
improves every time the ground moves.
